  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday Night through Thursday)\nIssued at 1043 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nAnother warm night is forecast Friday night with the pattern \nremaining nearly the same. Low temperatures are likely to bottom \nout in the 50s for most locations. Well above average temperatures\ncontinue on Saturday ahead of an approaching cold front and upper\nlevel storm system. Clouds will be increasing through the day as \nmoisture continues to advect into the region and lift increases. \nBy late afternoon or evening, showers and isolated thunderstorms \nwill begin to move into E OK ahead of the front. Precipitation \npotential then spreads areawide overnight into Sunday as the storm\nsystem passes over the FA. Instability continues to look quite \nweak in our area, especially as we move into the overnight period,\nso thunderstorm chances are likely to wane with time. Overall, \nrainfall amounts should still remain near or below a quarter inch \nfor most locations, though higher totals in excess of one half \ninch will be possible, especially with any thunderstorms. The \nheaviest rain totals are expected north of I-40.\n\nColder air will filter in behind the front as it moves through \nduring the day Sunday, likely complicating the high temperature \nforecast. Locations across W-Central AR and SE OK may have enough \ntime to warm into the 60s before the arrival of the front, but \nlocations in NE OK and NW AR are likely to remain in the 50s. From\nhere, continued cold air advection will promote low temperatures \nnear or below freezing through early next week, with highs perhaps\nstruggling to warm out of the 40s north of I-40.\n\nPlenty of disagreement remains with another system expected to \nmove into the plains mid next week, but it does appear poor \nmoisture will be a limiting factor. For now, odds favor mostly dry\nconditions, and PoPs remain below mentionable values. However, \nthis will be monitored given the colder temperatures in place. \nTemperatures begin to moderate by late next week.",
